 teach: {
  prompt: "Reconstruct, from the sodium channel upward, why a local anaesthetic often fails in a hot abscessed tooth and why pain disappears before the patient stops feeling pressure.",
  model: "Local anaesthetics are weak bases; only the uncharged form crosses the membrane, and inside it becomes charged and blocks the open sodium channel so threshold is never reached. Pus and inflammation make tissue acidic, pushing the drug toward its charged form outside the nerve, so less enters and the block is weak. Small-diameter fibres (C and A-delta, which carry pain) need less drug to block than thick A-beta touch fibres, so pain goes first while pressure sensation persists for longer."
 },
